# 4️⃣ Important Tree Terminology

## 🌱 Root

The topmost node of a tree.

```text
        10  ← Root
       /  \
      20   30
```

---

## 👨‍👩‍👧 Parent

A node that has one or more children.

```text
10
|
20
```

Here:

```text
10 = Parent
20 = Child
```

---

## 👶 Child

A node directly connected below another node.

```text
    10
   /
  20
```

20 is the child of 10.

---

## 🍃 Leaf Node

A node with no children.

```text
      10
     /  \
    20   30
```

Here:

```text
20 and 30 = Leaf Nodes
```

---

## 🔗 Edge

The connection between two nodes is called an edge.

```text
10
|
20
```

The line between 10 and 20 is an edge.

---

## 📏 Height

The height of a tree is the number of edges on the longest path from the node to a leaf.

Example:

```text
        1
       /
      2
     /
    3
```

Height:

```text
2
```

> Note: Some definitions count height in terms of nodes instead of edges. Always follow the convention used by the problem.

---

## 📊 Depth

Depth of a node is the number of edges from the root to that node.

```text
        1        Depth = 0
       /
      2          Depth = 1
     /
    3            Depth = 2
```

---

# 5️⃣ Types of Binary Trees

## 1. Full Binary Tree

Every node has either:

```text
0 children

OR

2 children
```

Example:

```text
        1
       / \
      2   3
     / \
    4   5
```

---

## 2. Complete Binary Tree

All levels are completely filled except possibly the last level.

The last level is filled from:

```text
Left → Right
```

Example:

```text
        1
       / \
      2   3
     / \  /
    4  5 6
```

---

## 3. Perfect Binary Tree

Every internal node has exactly two children and all leaf nodes are at the same level.

```text
          1
        /   \
       2     3
      / \   / \
     4   5 6   7
```

---

## 4. Balanced Binary Tree

A binary tree is balanced when the heights of the left and right subtrees remain sufficiently close according to the definition being used.

Balanced trees help maintain efficient operations.

---

## 5. Skewed Binary Tree

A tree where nodes mostly have only one child.

### Left Skewed

```text
    1
   /
  2
 /
3
```

### Right Skewed

```text
1
 \
  2
   \
    3
```

